there are five statewide ballot  questions on your ballot this year these  would make changes to nevada's  constitution  today we're going to tackle question  three it had to pass through the  legislative sessions  twice in 2017 and again in 2019 to get  on the ballot question three deals with  the state pardons board and the power it  has so first  what does the pardons board do it does  not erase a conviction but it will  restore certain rights to a pardon felon  such as serving on a jury and gun  ownership right now the pardons board is  made up of the governor  the attorney general and the supreme  court justices  added to the state constitution require  the board to meet  quarterly allow anyone on the board to  bring up items for discussion  right now just the governor can do that  removes a requirement for the governor  to vote yes  for a pardon to be granted that had  effectively served as a veto  the argument for is the board will meet  more frequently and timely  and a pardon would not just be on the  whims of the governor  the argument against is that the board  would meet even if there are not  qualified applicants and questioning why  we would take away the veto power of the  governor  when they have it over bills from the  legislature  now if question three is approved it  would be part of the state's  constitution on november 24th  we'll tell you about ballot questions  four and six  coming up at 6 30. and if you still have  some questions about how to vote we've  got you covered all you have to do is  head to  election 101 section right there on our  home page at eightnewsnow.com  and there you will find everything you  need to know
 

